          %h3#galois_connections Galois connections
          %h2#ExamplesInSetTheory Examples in set theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+function"} identity function
                on a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+the"} the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniqueness+quantifier"} unique
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                such that every element in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fixed+point"} fixed point
                of said function.
            %li
              %p
                In at least some of the “lower” levels of the hierarchy of countable ordinals, leading up to the Feferman-Schütte ordinal, fixed points of continuous operators on the first uncountable ordinal play a central role. More information may be found at
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+ordinal"} countable ordinal
            %li
              %p Critical points of elementary embeddings (non-fixed points)
          %h2#ExamplesInCategoryTheory Examples in category theory
          %h3#initial_algebras_and_final_coalgebras Initial algebras and final coalgebras
          %p
            Various classical fixed-point theorems for monotone functions on posets can be “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"}> categorified
            ” to give appropriate fixed-point theorems for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endofunctors"} endofunctors
            on categories. An example is that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleene%27s+fixed-point+theorem"} Kleene's fixed-point theorem
            generalizes to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ad%C3%A1mek%27s+fixed+point+theorem"} Adámek's fixed point theorem
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_3 Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              be a
